  "evidence": "TEXT_G: Pledges on economy and jobs (radical tax simplification; major public spending cuts; 1m skilled jobs via infrastructure/defence/nuclear; banking reform); NHS protections (no frontline cuts; restore free eye tests/dental check-ups); immigration controls (five‑year freeze, long‑term cap, points system); education and skills (school/university vouchers; expand grammar); pensions/welfare reform; transport and housing plans; energy costs focus (nuclear expansion, curb costly wind); law and order. TEXT_C: Heavy emphasis on immigration and Islam (halt all new immigration; deportations; bans on mosques/burka); EU exit and protectionism; radical law-and-order (corporal/capital punishment); restrict NHS/welfare to Britons; cultural identity measures; nationalise telecoms; defence retrenchment and foreign aid cuts.",
  "reasoning": "In 2010, voters’ top concerns were the economy/jobs, immigration, and the NHS; TEXT_G directly foregrounds broad economic recovery, job creation, immigration controls, and protecting frontline health services. TEXT_C focuses far more on identity and extreme law‑and‑order measures, offering a narrower and less mainstream response to the electorate’s core priorities.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_G"
}
